  "prompt_spec": {
    "original_prompt": "From Aristotle's works, it is clear that phantasia is causally downstream from aisthesis ontologically: phantasia ontologically depends on aisthesis but is different from it. Articulate the ontological chain of causal dependency, as aristotle understands it, of the following (each \"-->\" illustrates the following item being causally downstream): motion-->time-->aisthesis-->phantasia-->resonant aisthema and pathos -->phantasmata-->thinking, recollection, imagination, etc.",
    "research_questions": [
      "What is the ontological chain of causal dependency in Aristotle's psychology and natural philosophy, specifically tracing the sequence: motion → time → aisthesis → phantasia → resonant aisthema and pathos → phantasmata → thinking, recollection, and imagination?"
    ],
    "required_facets": [
      {
        "facet_id": "2dc538c2-1431-415e-aa4e-088a0c270f0d",
        "name": "Motion as Foundation for Time and Perception",
        "description": "How does Aristotle establish motion (kinesis) as the ontological foundation for time (chronos) in Physics Book IV, and how does this temporal framework enable the possibility of sense-perception (aisthesis)? This facet investigates Aristotle's argument that time is the measure or number of motion with respect to before and after, and examines how this metaphysical grounding of time in motion creates the necessary conditions for perceptual experience. Relevant passages include Physics IV.10-14 on time's dependence on motion, De Anima II.5 on the actuality of the sensible in act, and De Sensu on the temporal aspects of perception.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "e85d100b-db37-4963-9dfd-5af4445d06be",
        "name": "Aisthesis as Causal Prerequisite for Phantasia",
        "description": "What is the precise nature of the ontological and causal dependency of phantasia (imagination/appearance) on aisthesis (sense-perception) in Aristotle's De Anima III.3 and related texts? This facet examines Aristotle's explicit statements that phantasia cannot occur without prior aisthesis, that phantasia is a movement produced by actual sense-perception, and that phantasia differs from aisthesis in being capable of falsehood while actual perception of proper sensibles is always true. Key passages include De Anima III.3.428a-429a, De Memoria 450a-451a, and De Insomniis on the relationship between perception and phantasia in dreams.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "3abeaef5-1cd8-48a1-9e37-da57b9a926d3",
        "name": "From Phantasia to Resonant Aisthema and Pathos",
        "description": "How does Aristotle theorize the transition from phantasia to resonant or lingering sense-impressions (aisthema) and affective states (pathos) in De Memoria, De Insomniis, and Rhetoric? This facet investigates how phantasia produces or enables persistent sensory impressions that resonate beyond the initial perceptual encounter, and how these give rise to emotional and affective responses. Relevant texts include De Memoria on the persistence of sense-impressions as affections in the soul, Rhetoric II on how phantasia mediates emotional responses, and De Anima on the relationship between perception, phantasia, and pleasure/pain as fundamental pathē.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "07f1e751-17e1-4fe4-8d81-6c06e2f9ca2a",
        "name": "Phantasmata as Products and Cognitive Resources",
        "description": "What is the ontological status and causal role of phantasmata (mental images or appearances) as products of the phantasia-aisthema-pathos complex in Aristotle's cognitive psychology, particularly in De Anima III.7-8, De Memoria, and Metaphysics? This facet examines how phantasmata arise from the interaction of phantasia with resonant sense-impressions and affective states, and how these phantasmata serve as necessary resources for higher cognitive functions. Key passages include De Anima III.7.431a14-17 on thinking never occurring without phantasmata, De Memoria 450a-451b on phantasmata as objects of memory, and discussions of phantasmata in practical reasoning.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "4dea596c-65d9-4fd7-858a-ec199bedb7d4",
        "name": "Phantasmata Enabling Higher Cognitive Functions",
        "description": "How do phantasmata serve as the ontological foundation for thinking (noēsis), recollection (anamnēsis), and imagination (phantasia in its productive sense) in Aristotle's epistemology and psychology? This facet investigates the causal chain from phantasmata to various higher cognitive capacities, examining De Anima III.4-8 on intellection requiring phantasmata, De Memoria et Reminiscentia on recollection as a search through phantasmata, and Metaphysics on how universal concepts are abstracted from phantasmata. The focus is on how phantasmata function as necessary but not sufficient conditions for abstract thought, deliberate recollection, and creative imagination.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      }
    ],
